WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.189 align:start position:0%
 
be seated  after years of legal battles the  international court of justice ordered  colombia to immediately seize naval and  fishing activities in waters that the  court ruled belonged to nicaragua  by interfering with  fishing and marine scientific research  activities of  nicaraguan flag or nicaraguan licensed  vessels end with the operations of  nicaragua naval vessels in the republic  of nicaragua's exclusive economic zone  the republic of colombia has violated  the republic of nicaragua's  sovereign rights and jurisdiction in  this maritime zone  the ruling is the latest in a  century-old dispute between the two  countries over a number of islands in  the western caribbean that are home to  one of the world's largest coral reefs  nicaragua filed this case in 2013  after a previous 2012 ruling confirmed  colombia's sovereignty over the main  islands but reduce the expense of sea  belonging to colombia giving nicaragua  access to underwater oil and gas  deposits as well as fishing rights  but then colombian president juan manuel  santos said colombia could not agree to  changing its territorial boundaries  without a bilateral treaty a treaty that  has never been pursued  colombia's responsibility despite the  court orders colombia's current  president ivan duque insists it will not  allow nicaragua to limit any of what he  says or colombia's rights  we will defend colombia's highest  interests in the caribbean sea there is  no room for hesitation or ambiguity  faced with this kind of responsibility  some political observers say consecutive  colombian governments have tried to  avoid reaching a settlement fearing the  political consequences of any perceived  secession of sovereignty so the only way  to put an end to this today is via a  bilateral negotiation and the decision  today actually makes it quite  even more difficult to continue  postponing that now it remains to be  seen how the two countries sit down  given existing tensions between the  governments of nicaragua and colombia  colombia said it will not negotiate with  the government it considers a  dictatorship but the court's ruling are  final and legally binding which might  force them to do so potentially bringing  to an end this long-standing dispute  alessandro ampietti al jazeera bogota

